      "en": "Van Gogh's art helped him stay emotionally balanced. In 1885, he began work on what is considered to be his first masterpiece, *Potato Eaters*. His brother Theo, by this time living in Paris, believed the painting would not be well-received in the French capital, where impressionism had become the trend. Nevertheless, Van Gogh decided to move to Paris, and showed up at Theo's house uninvited. In Paris, Van Gogh first saw impressionist art, and he was inspired by the color and light. He began studying with Toulouse-Lautrec, Pissarro and others. Van Gogh was passionate, and he argued with other painters about their works, alienating those who became tired of his bickering. In February 1888, Van Gogh boarded a train to the south of France. He moved into the \"little yellow house\" and spent his money on paint rather than food. He lived off of coffee, bread and absinthe, and found himself feeling sick and strange, even sipping turpentine and eating paint. *(biography.com)*",
